{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh drew near, etc., and behold, Egypt, etc.</b> One must examine why it was necessary to say this, and what was said before it — ״וַיַּשִּׂיגוּ אוֹתָם״ etc. — did not suffice. And it further says ״וְהִנֵּה מִצְרַיִם נוֹסֵעַ״ etc.; also, why did it begin the cause with Pharaoh and finish the statement with Egypt, as it is written ״וְהִנֵּה מִצְרַיִם״ — should it not have said ״וְהִנֵּה פַּרְעֹה״ etc.? Further, one must examine its saying <b>נוֹסֵעַ</b> in the singular, etc., regarding Egypt, who are many." "To understand the verse, one must raise a great difficulty regarding the words of the children of Israel to Moses: ״הַמִבְּלִי אֵין קְבָרִים״ etc., ״מַה זֹּאת עָשִׂיתָ לָּנוּ״ etc. — for it sounds as though the matter were new to them, and this is not so, for HaShem had already informed them that He would harden his heart to pursue in order to be glorified through him, and He alerted them to this through the action He commanded them to perform — that they should turn back and encamp, etc. — in order to mislead him into pursuing after them, and they did so. They had thus prepared themselves to mislead him into pursuing them. If so, from where did the fear enter them? Even if they saw that he came with great force — six hundred chariots, etc. — which perhaps they had not previously thought he had such strength, nevertheless HaShem had already promised them that it is He who hardens his heart in order to be glorified through him, and they themselves had assisted in the matter." "<b>However,</b> it may be explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 21:5), who said as follows, and this is their language: ״וּמִצְרַיִם נוֹסֵעַ אַחֲרֵיהֶם״ — this is the prince of Egypt; מִצְרַיִם is his name. End of their language. Now, this angel came with them, for so is the practice, that the prince assists his nation and falls when it falls. Go out and learn from the story of Alexander of Macedon (Yoma 69b) that his prince would appear to him in the wars. Now, from the fact that the prince stands to the right of the king, not before the people — and behold, the order customary in wars is that the king is among them: the people go ahead of the king and the king is at the rear of the people. Here, the verse informs of the matter from which the sensation came to Israel to fear and be terrified, despite what had preceded for them from the prior announcement, and it says <b>וּפַרְעֹה הִקְרִיב</b> — meaning, he did not follow the usual order of placing the people first, but brought himself forward ahead of the people. <b>וַיִּשְׂאוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת עֵינֵיהֶם וְהִנֵּה מִצְרַיִם</b> — meaning, the prince of Egypt, as mentioned. The explanation is: because Pharaoh brought himself forward and the prince was standing before him, this was the reason Israel saw the prince whose name is מִצְרַיִם traveling, etc. — and they could not know the reason for the prince, that he was merely standing before the king as is customary, for it is not the practice of the king to go ahead of the camp; and they saw the prince traveling after them — meaning, ahead of the camp. Therefore they trembled, for they thought that celestial princes had come to wage war against them. And even though what had preceded them had preceded, it did not suffice for them when they saw angels coming to fight against them, for it is a sign that HaShem had retracted from His good intention — for otherwise He would not have allowed His angel to come against them, and therefore they cried out, etc. And had Pharaoh not gone first, or had it been the usual order in which the king goes first, they would have attributed the matter to the fact that he came not for war but to stand before the king, as is the law of celestial princes with all kings over whom they are appointed, and they would not have been afraid. And through this the matter is reconciled, and the verses are reconciled." "And the reason HaShem did not prevent the angel from coming is for two reasons: the first, in order to kill him before them, as it says ״וַיַּרְא יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת מִצְרַיִם מֵת עַל שְׂפַת הַיָּם״ — and they said of blessed memory (Zohar vol. II 52b) that this refers to the prince whose name is מִצְרַיִם. And a second reason: Elohim intended it for good, so that they would be frightened and return in complete repentance and draw their hearts toward heaven, so that through this merit HaShem would perform for them the great miracle of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — and this they had not been promised beforehand. And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 21:5) expounded on the word ״הִקְרִיב״ — and it did not say ״קָרַב״: it means that he brought the heart of Israel near to their Father in heaven, and so indeed it was, as it is written <b>וַיִּצְעֲקוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל ה׳</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:04.805280234Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}